            <div xml:id="d348">

                <pb facs="0575" n="461" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg461"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 Arms Truce/49: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-27T15:00:00-05:00">September 27, 1931—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>129. Your 167 September 26, 6 [<hi rendition="#italic">8</hi>] p.m. and 168
                    September 27, 11 a.m. In the hope of seeing a text which embodies the spirit of
                    an armaments <pb facs="0576" n="462" xml:id="pg462"/> holiday unanimously
                    adopted, we are prepared to acquiesce in the French version. You may so inform
                    the Third Committee and add that well before November 1st this Government hopes
                    to be able to make the declaration called for, subject of course to similar
                    action on the part of the principal Military and Naval Powers.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
